       1.3.4 Vulnerability analysis on Cacti version 1.2.22 - [completed] {Findings: Vulnerability: Cacti version 1.2.22 unauthenticated command injection vulnerability (CVE-2022-46169), Description: The vulnerability is in the `remote_agent.php` file within Cacti, which can be accessed without authentication. The `get_client_addr` function can be manipulated via HTTP headers to bypass the authorization check. The `polldata` action processes the `poller_id` parameter without proper sanitization, leading to command injection. Affected Versions: Cacti versions 1.2.22 and below, Severity: Critical (CVSSv3 score of 9.8), Exploitability: Exploits for CVE-2022-46169 are publicly available and have been observed in the wild. Proof-of-Concept (PoC) exploits and Metasploit modules exist. Impact: A successful exploit allows an unauthenticated attacker to execute arbitrary commands on the Cacti server, potentially leading to full compromise of the Cacti instance and the underlying system. Solution: Upgrade to Cacti version 1.2.23, 1.3.0, or later. For instances of 1.2.x running under PHP < 7.0, an additional specific change (a8d59e8) might also be required. References: NVD, Tenable, Sonar, Rapid7 Vulnerability Database, GitHub PoC repositories, Exploit-DB, Broadcom Inc., Qualys, Wazuh, Cyber Security Agency of Singapore.}
