2. Web application analysis - [completed]:{Findings: The /status page includes JavaScript that issues requests to proxy.response.htb/fetch with parameters 'url' and 'url_digest' (an HMAC over the URL). The proxy endpoint functions as an SSRF gateway but enforces a keyed hash to prevent tampering. The site exposes a /status/main.js.php endpoint.}
2.1. Analyze proxy functionality - [completed]:{Findings: proxy.response.htb/fetch rejects requests with tampered URLs showing "invalid url_digest", confirming server-side HMAC validation is required.}
3. HMAC oracle exploitation - [completed]:{Findings: /status/main.js.php can be abused as an HMAC-signing oracle by setting the PHPSESSID cookie to an arbitrary target URL; main.js.php returns the corresponding valid url_digest. Using these oracle-generated digests, authenticated SSRF requests were submitted via proxy.response.htb/fetch to internal hosts (notably chat.response.htb and api.response.htb), enabling retrieval of internal source code/configuration.}
3.1. Exploit PHPSESSID cookie for digest generation - [completed]:{Findings: Controlled PHPSESSID values submitted to /status/main.js.php produced valid HMAC digests for attacker-chosen URLs; those digests were used to craft signed requests through proxy.response.htb/fetch to enumerate and pull content from internal services.}

